        <h2>What is the difference between Pipedrive and HubSpot?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">Pipedrive is a sales CRM built around a visual deal pipeline; it is quick to adopt and keeps reps focused on moving deals. HubSpot is a customer platform where marketing, sales and service work from one shared database, so lifecycle, attribution and reporting can follow a buyer from first touch to renewal.</p>
        <p>We migrate teams from Pipedrive to HubSpot, so we have a view here. That is also why this comparison says plainly when you should not switch. Moving CRMs is real work, and it only pays off when the problem is one HubSpot actually solves.</p>

        <section id="data-model">
          <h2>How do the data models compare?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Pipedrive centres on deals, with people and organisations attached to them. HubSpot centres on the customer: contacts and companies carry lifecycle stages, marketing history, deals and tickets on one record. That difference matters once more than one team needs the same customer record.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Pipedrive</th><th>HubSpot</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Centre of the model</td><td>Deals and the pipeline</td><td>Contacts and companies across the whole lifecycle</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Who works in it</td><td>Mainly sales</td><td>Marketing, sales and service on the same records</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Lifecycle tracking</td><td>Focused on the deal stages</td><td>Lifecycle stages and lead status on every contact and company</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Service</td><td>Usually a separate tool</td><td>Tickets on the same customer record</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="marketing">
          <h2>What about marketing alignment?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">This is the clearest gap. Pipedrive's marketing features come as add-ons or connected tools, so campaign engagement often lives somewhere else. In HubSpot, forms, email, ads data and website activity land on the same contact record that sales works, which is what makes lead scoring and attribution trustworthy.</p>
          <p>If marketing and sales argue about lead quality, the cause is usually that they are looking at different records. One shared record, with <a href="/resources/hubspot-lifecycle-stages-explained/">lifecycle stages both teams agree on</a>, fixes most of that argument.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="reporting">
          <h2>How does reporting differ?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Pipedrive's sales reporting is clear and quick for a sales manager: deals, activity, conversion by stage. HubSpot can report across the full funnel, from first marketing touch through closed revenue and service, because the data sits in one place. The difference shows up when leadership asks which campaigns produced revenue.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="stay">
          <h2>When should you stay on Pipedrive?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Stay when your team is small and sales-led, marketing is light or handled elsewhere, and the main need is a pipeline reps will actually update. If your forecast and pipeline already work, a migration will cost more than it returns. Fix data hygiene and process first.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>One sales team, one simple pipeline.</li>
            <li>No need for shared marketing and sales reporting yet.</li>
            <li>The pain is rep discipline, not a disconnected customer record.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="switch">
          <h2>When is it time to move to HubSpot?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Move when your problems are about connection: several tools each hold part of the customer, marketing cannot show which campaigns produced revenue, handoffs between teams drop leads, or you need service and sales on the same account. Those are problems a shared platform solves; a better pipeline view does not.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Attribution and funnel reports are built in spreadsheets.</li>
            <li>Marketing and sales disagree on what a qualified lead is.</li>
            <li>Service or account management needs the full sales history.</li>
            <li>You are adding more tools to cover gaps around the CRM.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Weighing a move off Pipedrive?</strong> We will tell you honestly whether it is worth it. <button type="button" class="text-link js-open-hs-form">Book a free consultation</button></p></div>
        </section>

        <section id="migrate">
          <h2>What does moving from Pipedrive to HubSpot involve?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">The same steps as any CRM migration: audit what is in Pipedrive, map each field by meaning, deduplicate before import, test in a sandbox or test portal, rebuild automation as HubSpot workflows, then cut over and train. Pipedrive deals, people, organisations and activities map to HubSpot deals, contacts, companies and activities.</p>
          <p>Our <a href="/resources/salesforce-to-hubspot-migration-checklist/">Salesforce to HubSpot migration checklist</a> covers the full order, and the <a href="/resources/data-migration-mapping-sheet-pipelinesync/">data migration mapping sheet</a> lists the columns to fill in. See <a href="/solutions/hubspot-migration/">our HubSpot migration service</a> for how we run it.</p>
        </section>

